# Object-Oriented Programming

Java uses OOP to model real-world concepts.

## Classes and Objects
A class is a blueprint; an object is an instance. Use constructors to initialize fields.

```java
public class User {
    private String name; // Encapsulation

    public User(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}
```

## Inheritance and Interfaces
Inheritance (`extends`) allows creating child classes. Polymorphism lets you treat a child as its parent. Interfaces (`implements`) define a contract.

```java
public interface Runnable { void run(); }
public class Dog implements Runnable {
    @Override
    public void run() { System.out.println("Dog runs"); }
}
```
Enums group constants, e.g., `enum Status { ACTIVE, INACTIVE }`.

**Common Mistakes:** 
- Forgetting `@Override`, leading to accidental overloading instead of overriding.
- Trying to instantiate an interface or abstract class.

**Recap:** You understand state/behavior, inheritance trees, interfaces, and constant groupings (enums).

